I think you are confusing the client side and server side of Firebase. Firebase Authentication is a client-side service, so you can't directly use it on the server side (like in Laravel). But you can use Firebase Admin SDK on the server side to manage users and authentication.
However, if you want to allow guest users (anonymous users) to sign in, you can use the Firebase Authentication's anonymous sign-in feature. Here's how you can do it:
On the client side (e.g., using JavaScript or a mobile app), you can call the signInAnonymously() method to create an anonymous user. This will create a new user in Firebase Authentication with a unique ID, but without any authentication credentials.
On the server side (in Laravel), you can use the Firebase Admin SDK to verify the ID token of the authenticated user. The ID token is a JWT that contains the user's unique ID and other information. You can use the verifyIdToken() method of the Firebase Admin SDK to verify the token and get the user's information.
So, in your Laravel application, you would need to:
Install the Firebase Admin SDK for PHP. You can do this using Composer: composer require google/cloud-firestore
Initialize the Firebase Admin SDK with your project's service account credentials.
When a user signs in anonymously on the client side, they will receive an ID token. You can send this token to your Laravel server, and use the Firebase Admin SDK to verify it.
Once the token is verified, you can use the user's unique ID to manage their data in Firebase (e.g., in Cloud Firestore or Realtime Database).
Here's some example code for verifying an ID token in Laravel:
use Google\Cloud\Firestore\FirestoreClient;

// Initialize the Firestore client
$firestore = new FirestoreClient([
    'projectId' => 'your-project-id',
]);

// Get the ID token from the request
$idToken = $_POST['idToken'];

// Verify the ID token
try {
    $verifiedIdToken = $firestore->auth()->verifyIdToken($idToken);
    $uid = $verifiedIdToken->uid;
    // Use the UID to manage the user's data
} catch (Exception $e) {
    // Handle the error
}
Note that the Firebase Admin SDK's auth() method is used to access the Authentication service, and the verifyIdToken() method is used to verify the ID token.
